  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather continues through Saturday \nthough a few very isolated sea-breeze showers will occur this \nafternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure settles in today with drier air moving \nin. Radar is bearing out earlier guidance which hinted at a few\nrather isolated showers this afternoon, mainly focused along \nthe sea and bay breeze. So, kept some mainly slight chance POPs\nalong coastal strip into this eve. A large majority will stay \ndry however given the closed low/trough continuing to move away.\nAny showers dissipate early this eve with a quiet night\nunfolding. Temperatures warm some more to start the weekend \nwith high temperatures on Saturday right near average along with\ndry conditions. Guidance again hints that isoalated pop-up\nshowers could develop but signal is weak and so is forcing, so\nkept forecast mostly dry for now except perhaps an isolated\nconvergence shower in the Delmarva.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A frontal system on Sunday and Monday will \nbring the next opportunity for widespread showers and \nthunderstorms. Increasing confidence in potential heavy rain on \nMonday. \n\nHigh pressure weakens late Saturday and is replaced by \nadvancing low pressure from the Ohio Valley area. This low \nstrengthens and moves west of our area later Sunday and into \nMonday. This system will bring increasing chances for showers\nand thunderstorms later Sunday, with a bulk of the precipitation\ncoming moreso on Monday. The PWAT values off the forecast \nsoundings during these times show more 2+ inch PWAT values for \nour region, so it's possible that excessive rainfall may develop\nfor parts of the region. WPC did add a Slight Risk (2/4) for\nexcessive rainfall on Monday for the I-95 corridor and points\nnorth and west.  Moisture and instability will also increase \nahead of the front, so we likely will have to monitor the severe\nweather threat for the Sunday and Monday afternoon periods as \nwell. However, our area is not in any long term severe outlooks,\nand it is looking increasingly more likely that there are more \nhydro concerns with this system.\n\nGuidance continues to show this as a slow moving system with a\nfrontal boundary either stalling over our area or slowly pushing\nto south and east. This could result in another stretch of\nunsettled weather through next week. The current forecast does\nhave showers/thunderstorm chances each day next week, mainly in\nthe afternoon and evening. Important to note though that each\nday other than maybe Monday will have periods of tranquil\nweather but it does turn to look more active after Sunday.",
